<tr><td>Screening</td><td>Done by your managers or HR, as time allows</td><td>Background checks, drug screening and skills tests handled for you</td></tr>
<tr><td>Upfront cost</td><td>Job board fees, staff time, advertising</td><td>Hourly markup (temp) or placement fee (direct hire)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flexibility</td><td>Every hire is a permanent commitment</td><td>Temp, temp-to-hire or direct hire options</td></tr>
<tr><td>Payroll and paperwork</td><td>All on your team</td><td>Agency employs temporary staff and handles their payroll</td></tr>
<tr><td>Market insight</td><td>Limited to your own hiring history</td><td>Current pay and demand data from many employers</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<p>In-house hiring still makes sense for roles you fill rarely and have time to recruit for. For high-volume, urgent or specialized hiring, an agency usually wins.</p>
<p>Many Texas employers use both. They keep leadership and core roles in-house, then lean on an agency for seasonal peaks, new locations, high-turnover positions and the roles that keep sitting open. That mix keeps your internal team focused on the hires that shape the company while a partner handles volume and speed.</p>

