<h2 id="how-to-choose">How to choose a Texas staffing agency: a checklist</h2>
<p>Not all agencies work the same way. When you compare options, whether you found them by searching "staffing agency Texas," "Texas pro staffing," "top jobs staffing" or "job pro staffing," use this checklist to separate a real partner from a resume mill.</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Texas market knowledge.</strong> Ask which Texas cities and industries they recruit for and what pay rates they're seeing for your roles.</li>
<li><strong>A clear screening process.</strong> Ask exactly what they check: background, drug screening, skills assessments, references, work eligibility.</li>
<li><strong>Placement options.</strong> Make sure they offer the model you need, whether temporary, temp-to-hire or direct hire.</li>
<li><strong>Transparent pricing.</strong> Get markups, placement fees and conversion fees in writing before you sign.</li>
<li><strong>A replacement policy.</strong> Ask what happens if a placement doesn't work out in the first weeks.</li>
<li><strong>Responsiveness.</strong> Can you reach someone when a worker calls out at 6 a.m.? Look for after-hours availability.</li>
<li><strong>Fair treatment of candidates.</strong> Good agencies don't charge job seekers fees. How they treat workers affects who shows up for you.</li>
<li><strong>Onboarding support.</strong> Ask whether they help new workers with paperwork, orientation and first-day logistics.</li>
</ol>

<div class="callout callout--tip"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> give the agency a detailed job brief, including must-have skills, nice-to-haves, shift times, dress code and the real pace of the job. The more specific you are, the better the first round of candidates will be.</p></div>

